# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Nothing here is committed (.gitignore covers .env*).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Getting in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is one door and one password, printed inside the sponsored copy. Every
# reader types the same word; there are no accounts to sign in to and nothing
# to be approved for. Registering is a separate, optional thing: it tells the
# sponsor who received a copy and where (see District/Thana on /signup), and
# it is not what opens the library.
#
# Be clear-eyed about what this is. It is printed, in circulation, and
# identical in every copy of the run, so anyone who photographs the page can
# pass on what they saw. That is inherent to a static print run, and it is why
# nothing that matters hangs on it: the reader password can never administer
# the library no matter who types it.
SITE_PASSWORD=Exium

# The administrator's password. This one is NOT printed anywhere.
#
# Note the shape: the admin password is the reader password plus a character.
# That is how the sponsor wanted it, and it is why `lib/auth/config.ts`
# compares the admin password FIRST and with an exact `===`: a prefix match
# or a reader-first test would hand /admin to every reader.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=Exiumm

# Session cookie signing key. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it signs everyone out. Required in production; development falls
# back to a fixed insecure value so a fresh clone can be opened locally.
AUTH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The book files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PDFs are not in public/: a file with an extension is skipped by the proxy's
# matcher, which would make the gate on the pages decoration. They are served
# by `app/api/file/[slug]/route.ts`, which checks the same signed cookie and
# supports range requests.
#
# In development that route reads `private/books/<slug>.pdf` straight off disk.
# In production, point this at a GitHub Release (or R2 bucket) holding the same
# slug-named files and the route proxies the bytes instead.
#
#   BOOKS_RELEASE_BASE=https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/download/v1.0-books
BOOKS_RELEASE_BASE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reader registrations
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstash Redis / Vercel KV, for the optional sign-up records. Without these
# the app falls back to private/users.json, which is fine locally and does not
# work reliably on serverless.
#
# Do not paste a token into this file: it is committed. Set them in the
# hosting dashboard, or in .env.local, which is not.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
